[Music]  first on cbs this morning we're hearing  from a former facebook employee who says  the tech giant  has a diversity problem in its hiring  practices  rhett lindsay claims the predominantly  white leadership at facebook  has created a toxic work environment  focused on quotas based on race  separately the equal employment  opportunity commission is looking into  facebook's hiring methods  the social network tells cbs this  morning that increasing diversity at the  company  is a priority our national correspondent  jericha duncan is here with more on that  story jerica good morning michelle good  morning to you  lindsay worked at facebook for just  under a year he was hired  in 2020 to recruit diverse talent but  lindsey says he quickly found the  company's recruiting tactics  troubling the goal was to truly work at  a company where i could have great  impact  and i felt the time that facebook's  platform was the place where i can do  that  but when fully embedded in his new  position 32-year-old rhett lindsey says  he discovered what he calls  a problematic workplace and a culture  focused on quotas  it's the issue of not reaching out  because you're possibly the best fit for  the role  it's reaching out because you're a black  person and all i'm caring about is  trying to hit  my metric goal for the week on how much  outreach i've had to  diverse candidates and does that  translate though  into people getting jobs so  not necessarily and is that the problem  that's the problem  and what i would normally do is start  off by the skill set lindsey says he  reached a breaking point last  august when a song repeatedly using the  n word  was played during an online diversity  recruiting meeting  it goes back to leadership they deemed  that okay to play that in a diversity  recruiting meeting  where they be at we're talking about  trying to find black talent  and you're playing a song that says  where the at how hurt were you by that  i was extremely hurt i felt betrayed  by the company that i was proud to work  at  cbs news reviewed the video and saw  messages lindsey posted that everyone  could see  voicing his frustration in one message  lindsay wrote  why are you playing a song that is  literally saying the n word  come on i was leaving live comments that  the person that led the meeting could  see  people could see and there was people  leaving reactions to my comments  lindsay says his comments were not  addressed until the meeting ended  and the apology didn't come from the  white man who was playing the song  no it did not it came from a black woman  who was on that meeting  correct she spoke for the majority it  was  we apologize we're sorry in recent years  facebook has publicly pledged to put a  greater emphasis on diversity and  inclusion  the company's 2020 diversity report  shows the social network has been slowly  increasing representation over the past  seven years  from 2 percent of employees being black  to now  3.9 percent facebook tells cbs news it  plans to  increase the number of black people in  leadership roles in the u.s  by 30 percent by 2025.  still lindsey claims the hiring process  is flawed  due in part to what he says is  facebook's use of culture fit when  assessing candidates  the company denies using the practice  what is a  culture fit cultural fit is  identifying someone that blends well  with what's already established  evelyn carter is managing director at  paradigm  the strategy firm works with over 600  companies  seeking to diversify their workplaces  the problem is that culture fit  often turns into what some companies  literally call the beer test  right would i want to go out and have a  beer with you after work  that's a great criterion for finding a  new friend not so much for evaluating a  candidate for hire  since leaving facebook last november  lindsay started his  own consulting company with a focus on  diverse hiring  diversity is not just someone's skin  color and gender diversity is everyone's  background their experiences their  culture  their aspirations their identity are you  encouraged at all  by telling your story that facebook will  see this  and make changes my hope is that  facebook leadership  can reflect because it's my story uh  and my perspective but there's also  other people's perspectives  of their experiences at facebook that  they need to listen to  and the expert we spoke to says hiring  bias is not unique to facebook  and that we all have biases that we're  unaware of in a statement  facebook told cbs news they take  seriously allegations of discrimination  and have robust policies in place for  employees to report concerns and late  last night facebook  also told us they have reviewed the song  incident  and are taking appropriate action yeah  so many companies facing this issue  right now yes what's remarkable is that  song was  playing and he is in the chat saying  live what's up like who's doing this and  it was only after the meeting ended that  he had somebody respond to his concerns  correct all right jerica thank you

